correlation coefficient between \[X\] and \[Y\] (governing the strength of dependency).\(\mu_x, \mu_y\): The means of \[X\] and \[Y\].\(\sigma_x, \sigma_y\): The standard deviations of \[X\] and \[Y\].\[-\frac{2\rho (x-\mu _{x})(y-\mu _{y})}{\sigma _{x}\sigma _{y}}\]: The cross-product interaction term. This is what handles the correlation. If \(\rho = 0\) (independent), this entire term disappears, collapsing the formula precisely back into the independent model. 3. General Matrix Notation (Multivariate)When scaling this up to many variables, we represent the correlated model using a covariance matrix (\[\Sigma \]). \[\mathbf{X}\sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbfit{\mu },\mathbfit{\Sigma })\]Compared to an independent matrix where all off-diagonal numbers are zero, the correlated model populates those spaces with covariance elements (\[\sigma _{xy}\]): \[\mathbfit{\Sigma }_{\text{Independent}}=\left[\begin{matrix}\sigma _{x}^{2}&0\\ 0&\sigma _{y}^{2}\end{matrix}\right]\quad \text{vs.}\quad \mathbfit{\Sigma }_{\text{Correlated}}=\left[\begin{matrix}\sigma _{x}^{2}&\rho \sigma _{x}\sigma _{y}\\ \rho \sigma _{x}\sigma _{y}&\sigma _{y}^{2}\end{matrix}\right]\]Are you using this formula to simulate data points (like asset returns or synthetic user data), or are you writing out a likelihood function for a machine learning model? Knowing your programming language or end goal will help me provide the exact implementation.  Copied to clipboardFailed to copy to clipboard.  Try again later.CopiedFailed to copyCopyShare public linkThis public link shares a thread, which may include personal information. You can delete this link, but not copies made by others.Copy linkCan’t copy the link right now. Try again later.FacebookGmailXRedditWhatsAppShareGood responseBad responseMoreExport to DocsDraft in GmailAbout this responseSaved timeClearHelpfulComprehensiveOtherIncorrectInappropriateNot workingUnhelpfulOtherA copy of this chat will be included.A copy of this chat and the content you shared will be included.A copy of this chat and the content you shared will be included.SubmitThanks for letting us knowGoogle may use account and system data to understand your feedback and improve our services, subject to our Privacy Policy and Terms of Service. For legal issues, make a legal removal request.Shared0 filesShow lessShow allYou said: Can you write it in a compact form like E[Y|X] =CopiedCopyEditCan you write it in a compact form like E[Y|X] =10:55 AMAI Mode reply for Can you write it in a compact form like E[Y|X] =In a compact regression or conditional expectation framework, the correlated data model is written as: \[\mathbb{E}[Y\mid X]=\mu _{Y}+\rho \frac{\sigma _{Y}}{\sigma _{X}}(X-\mu _{X})\]If you normalize or center the data so that the means (\(\mu_X, \mu_Y\)) are zero, it simplifies directly to the familiar linear projection form: \[\mathbb{E}[Y\mid X]=\beta X\]Where the structural slope parameter (\[\beta \]) embeds the correlation coefficient:\[\beta =\rho \frac{\sigma _{Y}}{\sigma _{X}}\]Key Components of the Model\(\mathbb{E}[Y \mid X]\): The conditional expectation (the expected value or predicted baseline of \[Y\] given that we know the value of \[X\]).\[\rho \] (Rho): The core correlation coefficient driving the strength and direction of the dependency.\[\frac{\sigma _{Y}}{\sigma _{X}}\]: The scale adjustment factor (ratios of their standard deviations) that scales the units of \[X\] into the units of \[Y\].\((X - \mu_X)\): The distance or "deviation" of the observed \[X\] from its structural mean. If \[X\] and \[Y\] are completely independent, \(\rho = 0\), causing the entire right-hand adjustment term to disappear. The model collapses back to an independent state: \(\mathbb{E}[Y \mid X] = \mu_Y\). Are you planning to apply this conditional expectation model to timeseries forecasting, portfolio optimization, or a machine learning regression task? Knowing your specific application will help me provide the exact code or matrix extensions you need.  Copied to clipboardFailed to copy to clipboard.  Try again later.CopiedFailed to copyCopyShare public linkThis public link shares a thread, which may include personal information. You can delete this link, but not copies made by others.Copy linkCan’t copy the link right now. Try again later.FacebookGmailXRedditWhatsAppShareGood responseBad responseMoreExport to DocsDraft in GmailAbout this responseSaved timeClearHelpfulComprehensiveOtherIncorrectInappropriateNot workingUnhelpfulOtherA copy of this chat will be included.A copy of this chat and the content you shared will be included.A copy of this chat and the content you shared will be included.SubmitThanks for letting us knowGoogle may use account and system data to understand your feedback and improve our services, subject to our Privacy Policy and Terms of Service. For legal issues, make a legal removal request.Shared0 filesShow lessShow allwindow.fetchAsyncContentContainerReady && window.fetchAsyncContentContainerReady();You sent: 1 tab and said: Please help me download this R packageWarning message:In download.file(urls, destfiles, "libcurl", mode = "wb", ...) : URL 'https://cran.rstudio.com/bin/windows/contrib/4.6/pROC_1.19.1.zip': status was 'SSL connect error'Warning in download.packages(pkgs, destdir = tmpd, available = available, : download of package ‘pROC’ failedCopiedCopyEditcran.rstudio.comPlease help me download this R packageWarning message:In download.file(urls, destfiles, "libcurl", mode = "wb", ...) : URL 'https://cran.rstudio.com/bin/windows/contrib/4.6/pROC_1.19.1.zip': status was 'SSL connect error'Warning in download.packages(pkgs, destdir = tmpd, available = available, : download of package ‘pROC’ failedAI Mode reply for 1 tab and Please help me download this R package
